{"skill":{"slug":"clawvault","displayName":"ClawVault"},"version":{"version":"2.5.13","createdAt":1771202098326,"changelog":"Schema conformance pass: remove non-spec openclaw metadata keys, add source field, and keep only documented requirement/install/homepage fields for stable registry parsing.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"hooks/clawvault/handler.js","size":24735,"sha256":"4c2a5f73e2645edf1e72a66c5236d255468b63c413b70fd380b049d7308bf33e","contentType":"text/javascript"},{"path":"hooks/clawvault/HOOK.md","size":2866,"sha256":"e2720d1142d1a5a64d844505e2db5a25d71e7f27489fd44a01f045bdccaf99cc","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":12115,"sha256":"c5c9bbae7c35083ff0dd72eb7dfbf6c8daa06b87d38c605b7c5f66b7a15a2176","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2556,"sha256":"6c65d7ab6cb1333d5ac56688d6e239c196a039bf5f9f82882cbb8b528f2d3bbb","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789026466448,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"1891f0ab99177de6ff6ea66be9b67aa74b726adb215f178f0a7fc5b603b8aa34","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/1891f0ab99177de6ff6ea66be9b67aa74b726adb215f178f0a7fc5b603b8aa34","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: clawvault\nVersion: 2.5.13\n\nThe OpenClaw AgentSkills bundle for ClawVault is classified as benign. The `SKILL.md` transparently declares its capabilities, including reading/writing files in a vault directory, modifying OpenClaw session transcripts (with backups), and making LLM API calls for 'observe' compression using an optional `GEMINI_API_KEY`. The `hooks/clawvault/handler.js` implements these features by executing the `clawvault` CLI tool using `child_process.execFileSync` with `shell: false`, which is a secure method that prevents shell injection. Furthermore, the handler includes extensive input sanitization for all data passed to CLI commands or injected into agent prompts, demonstrating a strong focus on security. Any prompt injection against the agent is for benign purposes, such as providing context or a memory checklist, and is carefully sanitized.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160660143},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":69,"severity":"HIGH","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":7,"checkedAt":1789026466448},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This memory skill is mostly coherent, but enabling its hook gives it automatic access to prior sessions and vault memories in ways users should review carefully first.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The artifact discloses vault access, OpenClaw transcript access, and optional Gemini API use. The hook also scans active session metadata and can trigger observation on heartbeat, compaction, and new-session events, which is broader than a single user-invoked memory lookup.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation uses global npm packages, including clawvault latest and qmd from a mutable GitHub reference. That is expected for a CLI-backed skill, but it is not pinned to reviewed versions while the hook later invokes those binaries automatically.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The hook injects recovered recap and memory text into session messages with system-message authority. Restoring context is purpose-aligned, but treating prior user, assistant, or memory content as system-level context creates a trust-boundary issue because stored text can contain instructions.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The hook is opt-in and disclosed, but once enabled it persists across gateway lifecycle events and automatically runs recovery clearing, checkpointing, observation, context injection, and weekly reflection without per-event confirmation.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated memory, recovery, and context-restoration purpose matches most capabilities, including vault file writes, transcript repair with backups, checkpointing, observation, and weekly reflection. The concern is that the hook automatically processes OpenClaw session transcripts and vault memories, and observe features may use an external LLM provider when configured.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review and pin the CLI dependencies before installing, and only enable the hook if you are comfortable with it automatically reading OpenClaw session data, injecting recovered memories into agent context, clearing recovery state on startup, and possibly using an external LLM for observe compression when configured. Avoid storing secrets in transcripts or vault memories unless you have disabled or constrained observation and remote compression.","findings":"[AE1] unexpected: The local bundle includes SKILL.md, HOOK.md, and handler.js, and these were inspected; the partial-coverage warning was not treated as independent evidence of evasion.\n[SQP-2] expected: Automatic context injection is expected for this skill, but the documentation does not clearly warn that recovered prior-session and vault content can be inserted into agent-visible messages.\n[SQP-2] expected: Startup recovery with recover --clear is disclosed, but users are not strongly warned that enabling the hook automatically mutates and clears recovery state.\n[SDI-4] expected: The weekly reflection behavior is disclosed in HOOK.md and in the hook event list, so this finding was downgraded to a documentation consistency note rather than a hidden capability.\n[other] expected: Observation is part of the stated purpose, but handler.js automatically invokes observe --cron on lifecycle events and the documentation says observe compression can use an external LLM API, creating a sensitive-data exposure concern without per-transmission review.\n[T01] expected: Restored context is expected, but handler.js places transcript and memory-derived text into system-role messages, which can elevate untrusted stored content.\n[T08] unexpected: Dependency installation is expected, but mutable global installs are not necessary to the purpose and make it hard to know whether the installed binaries match the reviewed artifact.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789026466448}}}}}